# Chapter 2 – Technology Stack & System Architecture

---

# Technology Stack

Ravion DPR is built using a modern, scalable, and open-source technology stack that provides long-term maintainability, performance, and compatibility with future enterprise modules.

The technology selection emphasizes reliability, developer productivity, security, and ease of deployment.

| Layer                | Technology                                        |
| -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------- |
| Programming Language | PHP 8.3+                                          |
| Framework            | Laravel 12                                        |
| Database             | MySQL 8.x                                         |
| ORM                  | Laravel Eloquent                                  |
| Frontend             | Blade Templates                                   |
| CSS Framework        | Tailwind CSS                                      |
| JavaScript           | Vanilla JavaScript (Current), Alpine.js (Planned) |
| Authentication       | Laravel Authentication                            |
| Authorization        | Role-Based Access Control (RBAC)                  |
| Version Control      | Git                                               |
| Repository           | GitHub                                            |
| Web Server           | Apache / Nginx                                    |
| Local Development    | XAMPP / Laragon                                   |
| Deployment           | Linux VPS / Cloud Server (Planned)                |

---

# Software Architecture

Ravion DPR follows a layered architecture based on Laravel's MVC pattern.

```
Presentation Layer
        │
Blade Views
        │
Controllers
        │
Business Logic
        │
Models (Eloquent ORM)
        │
Database (MySQL)
```

Each layer has a clearly defined responsibility.

---

# Architectural Principles

The system follows the following principles:

## Modular Design

Every functional area is developed as an independent module.

Examples:

* Projects
* Labour
* Materials
* DPR
* Inventory
* Planning
* Reports

Each module should remain independent while integrating with shared master data.

---

## Master Data Driven

The application minimizes manual text entry.

Instead, users select data from standardized master records.

Examples include:

* Project
* Activity
* Labour Category
* Labour Type
* Material Category
* Material
* Brand
* Unit
* Vendor
* Contractor

This ensures consistency and improves reporting accuracy.

---

## Workflow Based

Every business transaction follows a controlled workflow.

Example:

Engineer creates DPR

↓

Submit

↓

PMO Review

↓

Approved / Rejected

↓

Reports

The same workflow philosophy will be applied to other modules such as Material Requests, Purchase Orders, and Planning.

---

## Role-Based Access

Users are granted access based on their assigned role.

Permissions are controlled centrally and determine:

* Menu visibility
* Screen access
* Create
* Edit
* Delete
* Approve
* Reports
* Administration

---

## Audit First

Every important business transaction is recorded in the Audit Log.

Tracked events include:

* Create
* Update
* Submit
* Approve
* Reject
* Activate
* Deactivate

Future versions may also log:

* Login
* Logout
* Password changes
* Export actions

---

# Folder Structure

The project follows the standard Laravel directory structure.

```
app/
│
├── Http/
│   ├── Controllers/
│   ├── Middleware/
│
├── Models/
│
├── Helpers/
│
├── Policies/ (Future)
│
└── Services/ (Future)
```

```
resources/
│
├── views/
│
├── layouts/
│
├── components/ (Future)
│
└── partials/
```

```
database/
│
├── migrations/
├── seeders/
└── factories/
```

---

# Coding Standards

The following coding standards shall be followed throughout the project.

## Controllers

Controllers should only coordinate requests.

Business logic should remain minimal and reusable.

---

## Models

Models define:

* Relationships
* Fillable fields
* Accessors
* Scopes
* Mutators

---

## Views

Views should contain presentation logic only.

Complex business calculations should never be performed inside Blade templates.

---

## Helpers

Shared utility functions such as Audit Logging shall be implemented through helper classes.

---

## Validation

All validation must occur inside Controllers (or Form Requests in future versions).

Validation messages should be user-friendly.

---

# Naming Conventions

## Controllers

```
ProjectController

MaterialController

LabourReportController
```

---

## Models

Singular names.

```
Project

Material

Vendor

LabourType
```

---

## Database Tables

Plural snake_case.

Examples:

```
projects

materials

material_categories

labour_types

vendors
```

---

## Primary Keys

Every table shall use:

```
id
```

as the primary key.

---

## Foreign Keys

Examples:

```
project_id

material_id

vendor_id

contractor_id

labour_category_id
```

---

# Database Design Philosophy

The database follows normalization principles.

Goals:

* Avoid duplicate data
* Maintain referential integrity
* Simplify reporting
* Improve scalability

Relationships use Laravel Eloquent ORM.

Examples:

```
Project
    hasMany DPR

Material Category
    hasMany Materials

Material
    belongsTo Material Category

Vendor
    belongsTo Material Category

Labour Category
    hasMany Labour Types
```

---

# Security Principles

The application follows the principle of least privilege.

Users receive only the permissions required for their responsibilities.

Sensitive operations such as approvals, configuration changes, and master management are restricted to authorized roles.

Future enhancements include:

* Two-Factor Authentication (2FA)
* Session timeout
* Password expiry policies
* Login audit trails

---

# Performance Strategy

The application is designed for efficient operation across multiple projects.

Performance considerations include:

* Eloquent eager loading
* Database indexing
* Pagination
* Lazy loading where appropriate
* Optimized queries
* Reusable components

Future enhancements:

* Redis caching
* Queue workers
* Background jobs
* Scheduled tasks

---

# Scalability Strategy

The architecture is designed to support future expansion without major structural changes.

Planned enterprise modules include:

* Inventory Management
* Procurement
* Purchase Orders
* BOQ
* Billing
* QA/QC
* Safety
* Equipment
* Asset Management
* Payroll Integration
* CRM
* Vendor Portal
* Customer Portal
* Mobile Application
* Odoo Integration

---

